====Reaction from the bodybuilding community====
Nakayama's entry into politics deeply divided the Sanese fitness community.

The "pro-business" faction, including gym owners, supplement company CEOs, and many of his former professional peers, was appalled. As entrepreneurs who "built their brands from nothing," they viewed his left-leaning policies—higher corporate taxes and pro-union rhetoric—as a direct threat to their livelihood. They largely saw him as a "class traitor" who used their industry to build his name only to turn on it.

Conversely, the "athlete" faction—the much larger audience of amateur trainees, fitness influencers, and his "Ren-Fit" followers—viewed him as a hero. This group, often working low-wage jobs to support their training, felt he was the first politician who "gets it." When Nakayama spoke about the [[National Universal Health Act]], they heard a way to treat training injuries without going bankrupt. When he attacked inflation on groceries, they saw a leader who understood the high cost of a bodybuilder's diet.

===Acting career===
[[File:Rento Nakayama Morning Breeze.png|thumb|right|250px|Rento Nakayama in ''Morning Breeze''.]]
After graduating in 2015, Nakayama began a moderately successful acting career, primarily in television. He was best known for his role as "Kenji," a kind-hearted barista, in the popular KTK morning drama ''Morning Breeze''. He also gained recognition for his recurring role as Detective Morita in the action series ''Haru City Police''. His acting career effectively ended in 2021 as he began to focus more on his fitness brand and burgeoning political interests.

